# Windows scripts keep their bytes, exactly as they are.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is on by default on Windows, which normalises CRLF to LF going
# into the repository and back on the way out. That is right for source, and
# wrong for these three:
#
#   - a `.cmd` file with LF line endings is a Windows hazard in its own right,
#     and these are the three double-clickable files the whole app is opened
#     with;
#   - `publish-check` now inspects these files byte by byte, because a stray
#     carriage return inside a comment silently ended the comment and turned the
#     rest of the sentence into a command — a bug that shipped in every release
#     from v0.2.1. A guard that reads the bytes should read the bytes that are
#     actually stored;
#   - and it keeps a one-byte fix looking like a one-byte fix. Without this, the
#     repair to that carriage return was a 90-line diff, which is exactly how a
#     real change hides.
#
# `-text` means "no conversion in either direction": what is committed is what
# was written, on every machine whatever its git is configured to do.
*.ps1 -text
*.cmd -text
*.bat -text
